<.part title="What a Tool is" id="tools-what">
  <p>
    A Tool is a ready-made way of working that somebody on this server has packaged up:
    a board with its lists, folders, tags, wiki pages and rules already in place, the
    skills that tell your AI assistant how to use that board, and the docs that explain
    it. Job Search, an automated job search, is one.
  </p>
  <p>
    You'll find the Tools you can use under
    <.link navigate={~p"/tools"} class="link">Tools</.link>
    in the menu: your own, the ones shared with you, and the ones listed for everyone here.
  </p>
</.part>

<.part title="Installing one" id="tools-install">
  <p>
    Open a Tool and pick the assistant you use — Claude on the website or app, Claude
    Code, or ChatGPT — and follow its steps. They come to the same thing: connect your
    assistant to Slipdock, add the Tool's skills to it, and then start a chat and say
    <strong>“Set up …&nbsp;for me.”</strong>
  </p>
  <p>
    The assistant then asks Slipdock to build the Tool's board, which it does the same
    way for everybody, and talks you through filling it in. You can also build the board
    yourself with <strong>Make the board</strong> on the Tool's page. Either way you get
    one board per Tool: asking again finds the board you already have.
  </p>
</.part>

<.part title="Before you install" id="tools-safety">
  <p>
    A Tool's skills are instructions your assistant follows with your access to your
    boards. So the Tool's page shows everything before you install it: the board it will
    build, the files it adds, and every skill file in full. Read them as you would
    anything you let act for you.
  </p>
  <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-5">
    <li>Installing touches only the new board it makes, never your other boards.</li>
    <li>A published version never changes. A new version has a new number.</li>
    <li>
      A Tool listed for everyone has had that version approved by an admin; a newer
      version isn't shown to you until it has been approved too.
    </li>
  </ul>
</.part>

<.part title="New versions" id="tools-upgrade">
  <p>
    When a Tool you've installed has a newer version, its page offers <strong>Upgrade the board</strong>. Upgrading adds what your board is missing — new
    lists, folders, tags, pages, rules and files — and leaves everything you've changed
    exactly as it is. A page you've edited is never overwritten. Update the skills the
    same way you added them.
  </p>
</.part>

<.part title="How your board plans: Principles and Playbook" id="tools-playbook">
  <p>
    The built-in handbook Tool gives a board two pages that say how its work is
    decided and planned. <strong>Principles</strong>
    are the tie-breakers: where a change could go two right ways, which one this
    project takes. Each is a short paragraph and a matching <code>PRIN</code>
    rule, which agents keep to and a verifier checks. It starts with three
    that suit most projects (one place for each concern, the obvious reading of an
    ambiguous ask, extend before adding and split what has grown).
  </p>
  <p>
    The <strong>Playbook</strong>
    is what an agent follows when you ask it to plan ("I want to build…", "break
    this into cards"): it sizes the ask as a card, a feature or a system, then runs
    the stages that size takes (grill, decide, principles, spec, cards), each
    writing to a handbook page or to cards. A one-sentence ask stays one card.
    Its Defaults line can name a page of your own stack and hosting choices;
    those are proposed and asked about, never imposed.
  </p>
  <p>
    Editing these two pages is how a board changes its process: an agent reads
    them as they are now. A new principle is a rule, so an agent proposes it and
    you decide. Installing the Tool again replaces a page that still holds the
    first, empty version with this baseline, and never touches one you've edited.
  </p>
</.part>

<.part title="Publishing your own" id="tools-publish">
  <p>
    Put the Tool in a folder (see the next part) and publish it with the <.link
      patch={~p"/help/agents/cli"}
      class="link"
    >CLI</.link>:
  </p>
  <.snippet id="help-tools-publish" text="slipdock tools publish ./my-tool" />
  <p id="tools-publish-upload">
    Or, without the CLI, pack the folder as a <code>.tar.gz</code>
    (<code>tar czf my-tool.tgz my-tool</code>) and upload it under <b>Publish a Tool</b>
    on the Tools page. The name in its <code>tool.json</code>
    decides whether it is a new Tool or the next version of one of yours; your Tool's own
    page takes its next version too.
  </p>
  <p>
    Its page then appears under Tools for you alone. Publish again with a higher
    <code>version</code>
    for each change; the same version twice is refused.
  </p>
  <p id="tools-download">
    To start from a Tool that is already published, yours or one you can see, take
    the whole package — every file at its path — with <b>Download the package</b> on its page, or:
  </p>
  <.snippet id="help-tools-download" text="slipdock tools download my-tool" />
  <p>
    Unpack it, change what you need, raise the <code>version</code>
    in <code>tool.json</code>, and publish it again, with the CLI or by uploading it. Somebody else's Tool keeps its
    name, so publish a copy under a new <code>name</code>.
  </p>
</.part>

<.part title="The package" id="tools-format">
  <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-5">
    <li>
      <code>tool.json</code>: <code>name</code>
      (lowercase, unique on this server), <code>version</code>
      (like 1.0.0), <code>summary</code>, and optionally <code>title</code>, <code>author</code>,
      <code>requires</code>
      and <code>files</code>.
    </li>
    <li><code>README.md</code>: the docs, shown as the Tool's page.</li>
    <li>
      <code>board.json</code>: the board — <code>lists</code>
      (each with a <code>role</code>:
      ready, backlog, doing, done or dropped), <code>folders</code>, <code>tags</code>,
      <code>pages</code>
      (skeletons, which may use <code>{"{{board.name}}"}</code>
      and <code>{"{{today}}"}</code>) and <code>automations</code>
      (ready-made rules by
      preset name; a rule that sends cards to a runner can carry the <code>prompt</code>
      its jobs are given).
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>skills/&lt;name&gt;/SKILL.md</code>, with front matter naming the skill and saying
      when to use it, and anything else the skill needs beside it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>files/</code>: each listed in <code>tool.json</code>
      as going on the <code>board</code>
      (into a Tool folder in its wiki) or <code>local</code>
      (beside
      a skill, on the installer's machine).
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Slipdock checks all of it when you publish and says exactly what is wrong — a list
    meant to be the ready list that wouldn't be read as one, a rule on a list that
    doesn't exist, a file not listed in <code>tool.json</code>.
  </p>
  <p id="tools-files-at-run-time">
    A skill can fetch any file of its Tool while it runs, by its path in the package
    (<code>/api/tools/&lt;name&gt;/files/skills/&lt;skill&gt;/SKILL.md</code>, or
    <code>list_tools</code>
    with <code>file</code>), from the newest version you can see. So a short skill can
    read its steps from the server and follow a new version without being installed
    again. A Tool's board files are read from the board's Tool folder with <code>/api/boards/&lt;board&gt;/files/Tool/…</code>.
  </p>
</.part>

<.part title="Sharing and listing" id="tools-listing">
  <p>
    On your Tool's page, share it with people by email; they can read it and install it.
    To offer it to everyone on this server, ask an admin to list it. Admins approve one
    version at a time, under <strong>Tool listings</strong>, so each new version is
    approved before it reaches anyone you haven't shared it with.
  </p>
</.part>

<.part title="Skills from elsewhere" id="tools-skills">
  <p>
    Skills are also published outside Slipdock, often as a GitHub repository of
    <code>SKILL.md</code>
    files. Under <.link navigate={~p"/skills"} class="link">Skills</.link>
    in the menu, add one by its URL: a repository, a folder or one file in one, or a
    <code>SKILL.md</code>
    at any https address. Slipdock fetches each skill in it (or the README, when there
    are none) and keeps it with its name, description, version, licence and when it
    was fetched. Open a skill to read the whole file before anything follows it.
  </p>
  <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-5">
    <li>
      Only public addresses are fetched, redirects aren't followed, and a file over the
      size limit is left out. What was left out, and why, shows under the source.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Refresh</strong>
      fetches the source again and says which skills were added, changed or removed;
      <strong>Remove</strong>
      deletes it and its skills.
    </li>
    <li>
      Your sources are yours alone. The ones listed under <em>On this server</em>
      are there for everyone, and only an admin changes them.
    </li>
    <li id="tools-skills-seeds">
      A new server starts with one, <code>mattpocock/skills</code>, enabled on no board.
      An admin adds another by ticking <em>For everyone on this server</em> when adding it.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p id="tools-skills-enable">
    To give a skill to a board's agents, open it and <strong>Enable this text</strong>
    on a board you can write to. The board keeps the text you read: a refresh that
    changes the skill shows <strong>Changed upstream</strong>
    with the lines that differ, and the board's agents keep the old text until somebody
    who can write to the board reads the change and re-pins it. A stage of a board's
    Playbook asks for a skill with a <code>skill: &lt;name&gt;</code>
    line; the Skills page lists those stages and flags one whose skill nothing provides.
  </p>
</.part>
